Yes. With the scope you just described, I think the advisor estimate is simultaneously correct and much too pessimistic for what you actually need.

The 31–56 engineer-month estimate in `docs/planning/ubuntu-derived-full-distribution-roadmap.md` is an estimate for a **supportable public distribution**: independent archive operations, security response, broad installer cases, real hardware qualification, your own newest-kernel release lane, recovery, signing infrastructure, release engineering, support commitments, and repeatable upgrades over years. You do not need most of that to make TemplarOS useful on your own machine.

For the next month I would explicitly redefine the target as:

> **TemplarOS Developer Edition 0.1:** a reproducibly generated, Ubuntu 26.04-derived amd64 system for one known Intel + RTX 4060 laptop, optimized for Rust/CUDA development, heavy multitasking, browsing and media, with a small Templar package overlay, a recoverable Btrfs root, working NVIDIA/CUDA, and enough installation machinery to recreate the machine from scratch.

That is a very different project.

## Where the project actually is

There is substantially more foundation here than the advisor's percentage makes it sound like.

The pack contains about **132,700 lines of Rust already**, plus roughly 20,000 lines of configuration/docs/interfaces. More importantly, several difficult architectural pieces aren't hypothetical anymore:

* `lege dev` has real build-set identity, local signed repository handling, pinned installation, rollback, activation rules, and installed-versus-running convergence reporting.
* `lege-hostd` already contains a boot deployment abstraction, Btrfs snapshot machinery, update transaction states and GRUB/systemd-boot backends.
* `lege-boot-health` can now actually bless a healthy trial deployment or request rollback of an unhealthy one.
* you have the component matrix in `distro/deploy/components.toml`.
* hardware qualification is already defined specifically for the Intel/NVIDIA laptop.
* the current image skeleton is mkosi + UEFI + Btrfs.
* Debian packaging exists.
* the shared file index has a unusually well-developed design before implementation.
* the distro roadmap already made the important architectural decision: **binary derivative of Ubuntu, not an Ubuntu rebuild**.

There is also an important wrinkle I found while inspecting this particular agent pack. The pack generator says it omitted **54 "unsupported/non-development" files**. The archive contains generated `distro/image/mkosi.conf.d/50-seeds.conf`, but not the `.seed` inputs it says generated it; likewise several ordinary Debian files and extensionless distro data appear absent. I would *not* conclude the actual repository lacks them—the more likely explanation is that the agent-pack allowlist rejects `.seed`, extensionless files such as `os-release`, and some Debian packaging suffixes.

**Fix the agent-pack generator before unleashing six agents on distro engineering.** Otherwise they will be reasoning from a selectively incomplete distro tree. This is a 30-minute class of problem with outsized consequences.

---

# The architectural shortcut I recommend

For TemplarOS 0.1, deliberately **do not build a miniature Canonical**.

Use Canonical for everything that does not distinguish TemplarOS:

**Ubuntu owns:**

* glibc
* systemd
* apt/dpkg
* coreutils
* kernel **initially**
* shim
* GRUB
* Mesa
* firmware
* NetworkManager
* PipeWire
* Bluetooth stack
* NVIDIA kernel modules initially
* CUDA packages initially
* security fixes to all of those

**TemplarOS owns:**

* compositor/session/shell
* system services you have already designed
* Files and the shared index
* app identity
* resource management
* defaults
* package selection
* Templar packages
* image recipe
* local/release overlay archive
* your Btrfs deployment/update mechanism
* your particular machine's qualification
* eventually installer/ISO
* eventually kernel, **only if there is a demonstrated reason**

This is what makes the one-month version plausible.

Ubuntu 26.04 is particularly convenient for you because CUDA itself is now in the Ubuntu archive: `cuda-toolkit` exists in Resolute, currently at CUDA 13.1.1. ([Ubuntu Packages][1]) NVIDIA's current CUDA 13.3 documentation also explicitly lists Ubuntu 26.04 x86-64 as a qualified platform. ([NVIDIA Docs][2])

So **CUDA no longer requires turning NVIDIA's repository into another distribution authority**.

That simplifies your distro considerably.

---

# I would reverse one part of the current roadmap

For your immediate goal, **do not make Linux 7.2 a requirement for TemplarOS 0.1.**

As of September 3, 2026, Linux 7.2.3 is the current stable kernel and 7.3-rc1 is mainline. ([Kernel.org][3]) It is perfectly reasonable to build a 7.2.3 experimental lane.

But your machine is:

* Intel iGPU
* RTX 4060 Laptop GPU
* Wayland
* CUDA
* hybrid graphics

That is exactly the kind of machine where turning the kernel into an independently maintained component gives you disproportionate pain.

Ubuntu's current Resolute kernel already has matching `linux-modules-nvidia-580-open-generic` packages; the current generic LRM tracks the Ubuntu 7.0 ABI. ([Ubuntu Packages][4])

The best initial policy is therefore:

| Channel        | TemplarOS 0.1                                           |
| -------------- | ------------------------------------------------------- |
| `stable`       | Ubuntu 26.04 GA/HWE kernel + Ubuntu matching NVIDIA LRM |
| `rescue`       | previous known-good Ubuntu kernel                       |
| `candidate`    | 7.2.3 Templar kernel experiment                         |
| `experimental` | 7.3 RC, if you feel like testing it                     |

The distinction from the advisor roadmap is simply that **Ubuntu's kernel remains stable instead of rescue for the first personal release**.

Move 7.2 to stable only after it offers you something you can name.

Faster compilation? Almost certainly not meaningfully.

Better scheduler behavior? Measure it.

Hardware support you need? Then it becomes justified.

Interesting technical project? Fine—but don't put it in the critical path.

That change alone removes a huge amount of the advisor's 5–9 engineer-month kernel/driver/Secure-Boot workstream from your one-month target.

---

# Secure Boot: take the boring route

I think ADR-0004 should be resolved for now as:

> **Canonical-signed shim → Canonical-signed GRUB → Ubuntu-signed kernel → Canonical-signed matching NVIDIA LRM.**

Ubuntu documents this as its normal amd64 Secure Boot chain. Shim is Microsoft-signed, GRUB and the normal kernel are Canonical-signed, and kernel modules are signature-enforced. ([Ubuntu Documentation][5])

That gives you actual Secure Boot while doing very little security engineering yourself.

Do **not** make "LegeOS-signed systemd-boot" your first production boot path.

Your current mkosi systemd-boot setup is fine for VM/dev images. Bare metal should use GRUB initially.

Later, if you qualify your own 7.2 kernel, enroll a Templar MOK and use it for the custom kernel/modules. Ubuntu's shim/MokManager infrastructure explicitly supports enrolling your own keys for unofficial kernels and modules. ([Ubuntu Documentation][5])

That is still work, but it isn't "become a Secure Boot platform vendor."

---

# You also do not need an installer yet

This is another enormous scope reduction.

A public distribution needs:

* live environment
* polished graphical installer
* empty disk
* dual boot
* encryption
* weird partition tables
* offline installation
* non-US keyboards
* accessibility
* failed installation recovery
* OEM mode
* NVIDIA detection
* arbitrary disk sizes
* lots of firmware

**Your personal distro doesn't.**

You know the hardware.

You know the desired disk layout.

You know the user.

You know that it is UEFI.

You can define one supported installation.

So TemplarOS 0.1 should initially have a **reproducible target-machine provisioner**, not a general installer.

I would support these two installation paths:

1. **mkosi raw image** for VM/debugging.
2. **Ubuntu 26.04 autoinstall/bootstrap → Templarize** for bare metal.

The second can eventually be wrapped into your own ISO. For now, an official Ubuntu installation environment can create the base installation, after which one Templar bootstrap step:

* configures Btrfs/subvolumes;
* adds the Templar local/archive source;
* installs `templaros-desktop`;
* installs your session;
* selects NVIDIA profile;
* installs CUDA developer packages;
* configures GRUB;
* sets OS identity;
* creates the user;
* enables required services;
* runs `templar doctor --install`;
* reboots.

Once that is fully declarative, **making an ISO around it becomes packaging**, rather than discovering what your installer is supposed to do.

---

# What "lighter than Lubuntu" should mean

Don't optimize around ISO size.

Lubuntu 26.04.1's current ISO is about 3.7 GB. ([cdimage.ubuntu.com][6]) That's interesting, but it tells you little about runtime responsiveness.

Your founding document had the better definition: lightness should mean responsiveness.

I would measure TemplarOS with five numbers:

| Metric                           |                              Suggested target |
| -------------------------------- | --------------------------------------------: |
| Idle memory after login          | **<700 MB** initially; push toward 450–600 MB |
| Templar-owned idle CPU           |                                effectively 0% |
| Boot → usable Home/session       |            <8–12 s after firmware on your SSD |
| Background write I/O at idle     |                            approximately zero |
| Always-running desktop processes | preferably <20 Templar/user-session processes |

Don't contort the system to hit arbitrary RAM numbers. A 48 GB development machine is harmed more by a 1% CPU polling loop or constant filesystem rescanning than by a 40 MB daemon that sleeps.

## Things I would leave out

For **your personal build**, not a public distribution:

* LibreOffice
* email client
* calendar
* contacts
* graphical software store
* Snap
* Ubuntu reporting
* Apport/Whoopsie
* games
* printer stack
* scanner stack
* modem support
* Samba server
* remote desktop server
* GNOME stack
* LXQt stack
* KDE stack
* generic desktop settings daemons
* multiple terminals
* multiple file managers
* desktop widgets implemented as separate daemons
* content indexing
* thumbnail daemons running continuously
* automatic discovery daemons you don't use
* redundant network applets
* redundant update applets
* generic OEM support
* legacy BIOS support
* arm64
* TPM FDE initially
* multiple bootloaders in the installed system
